From 28208dcbd77cb4ca42b42abf0a8e20956d88967d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anuj Gupta <anuj20.g@samsung.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 15:20:53 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 11/18] dma-buf: do not leak ctx on ctx_release_work's
 WARN_ON_ONCE paths

dma_buf_io_ctx_release_work(), added by patch 1 of "Add dmabuf read/write
via io_uring", ends with two "cannot happen" checks:

	if (WARN_ON_ONCE(ret <= 0))
		return;
	if (WARN_ON_ONCE(rcu_dereference_protected(ctx->map, true)))
		return;

	if (refcount_dec_and_test(&ctx->refs))
		dma_buf_io_ctx_destroy_work(&ctx->release_work);

Either WARN firing returns before the reference is dropped. A WARN is a
"this should never happen" marker, not licence to leak: hitting either
path permanently leaks ctx, the dma_buf reference it holds, the driver
attachment, and now the target file this ctx pins.

The two conditions are not equally unreachable. dma_resv_wait_timeout()
is called here with intr=false and timeout=MAX_SCHEDULE_TIMEOUT, and our
fence never implements a custom ->wait op, so dma_fence_default_wait()
can only return once the fence's SIGNALED bit is set -- ret <= 0 is
provably unreachable for our own fence under these parameters (it could
in principle still fire if this dma_buf has some other, unrelated fence
attached at DMA_RESV_USAGE_KERNEL with a misbehaving custom ->wait, which
this code has no way to rule out). The second condition is not similarly
provable: nothing here marks ctx as closing to new imports, so a
concurrent dma_buf_io_create_map() can legitimately republish a fresh map
to ctx->map between drop_map()'s unlock and this wait returning.

That second case is exactly why this commit must come after "dma-buf:
split map pointer and DMA-active lifetimes", not before it. Once a map
exists, safely falling through to drop the reference here depends on
that map holding its own pin on ctx -- dma_buf_io_init_map()'s
unconditional refcount_inc(), which that map's own software kref keeps
alive for as long as anything holds a pointer to it, dropped only once
the map's last reference is released. Before that invariant existed,
falling through unconditionally on either WARN would have risked
dropping ctx's reference while a live map's ctx pointer was still in
use -- a straightforward use-after-free waiting to happen, not a
warning, so the only safe response then would have been to leak rather
than free. That is the bug this commit exists to fix. With the invariant
in place, warn on both conditions but fall through to the reference drop
unconditionally: ctx cannot reach zero out from under a live map,
because that map is itself holding a reference.

Signed-off-by: Anuj Gupta <anuj20.g@samsung.com>
---
 drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c | 12 ++++++++----
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c b/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c
index 432cf97cf2ab..1c71d64057b7 100644
--- a/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c
+++ b/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c
@@ -236,10 +236,14 @@ static void dma_buf_io_ctx_release_work(struct work_struct *work)
 	ret = dma_resv_wait_timeout(dmabuf->resv, DMA_RESV_USAGE_KERNEL,
 				    false, MAX_SCHEDULE_TIMEOUT);
 
-	if (WARN_ON_ONCE(ret <= 0))
-		return;
-	if (WARN_ON_ONCE(rcu_dereference_protected(ctx->map, true)))
-		return;
+	/*
+	 * Both of these are "cannot happen" conditions, but an early return
+	 * here would skip the put below and permanently leak ctx, the
+	 * dma_buf reference it holds, the driver attachment, and now the
+	 * target file. Warn and still drop the reference rather than leak.
+	 */
+	WARN_ON_ONCE(ret <= 0);
+	WARN_ON_ONCE(rcu_dereference_protected(ctx->map, true));
 
 	dma_buf_io_ctx_put(ctx);
 }
